# Slurm job builder

Start with one job and add config files or matrix values only when needed.
`plan` and `render` work on every supported OS; only `submit` needs `sbatch`.
Generated jobs target Bash and Slurm.
Generated jobs may also need configured module, Conda, GPU, `srun`, `git`, or
`flock` tools. Choose the `action` in the dashboard or as the first positional
argument.

> **Configuration safety:** Review every rendered batch script before
> submission. Do not put secrets in TOML variables because they are written
> into those scripts.

Generated scripts keep the useful safety checks from the former Bunya script:
optional Conda activation and scratch caches, GPU checks, one-time locked
preparation, and a branch guard. Jobs verify a requested branch but never run
`git checkout`, so parallel submissions cannot race over one worktree.

## TOML layout

The smallest useful file is:

```toml
[[jobs]]
command = "python train.py"
```

`name` defaults to `job-1`, `workdir` defaults to the TOML directory and must
already exist, and a job with no expansion keeps its exact name. Expanded jobs
append config and matrix labels, while collisions receive a numeric suffix.
Every job writes `<job-name>.out` and `<job-name>.err`.

Commands may be shell-style strings or arrays of argument strings. Strings are
split into arguments without invoking a shell. Arrays run Python, compiled
C++, or any other executable without reinterpretation:

```toml
command = ["./simulation", "--threads", "4"]
```

Use an explicit shell only when shell syntax is required:

```toml
command = ["bash", "-lc", "make && ./simulation | tee result.txt"]
```

Each `configs` glob is resolved from `workdir` and combined with every matrix
value. Placeholders supported in `command` and `prepare` are `{config}`,
`{branch}`, `{workdir}`, and every matrix key. Add more `[[jobs]]` tables for
unrelated commands. The default `--limit 1000` catches accidental expansions
before submission. With `prepare_once = true`, a lock and success marker
derived from the prepare command lets only the first job prepare shared input.
Remove the matching `.slurm-prepare-*.done` marker to prepare again.

`[cluster]` accepts `account`, `partition`, `qos`, `gres`, `constraint`,
`nodes`, `ntasks`, `ntasks_per_node`, `cpus_per_task`, `mem`, and `time`.
Only `nodes`, `ntasks_per_node`, and `cpus_per_task` default to `1`; omitted
site-specific fields are left to Slurm.

`[environment]` accepts `modules`, `conda_env`, `scratch_base`, `check_gpu`,
`launcher`, and a nested `variables` table. The launcher defaults to `["srun"]`
and may be `[]` to run commands directly. Conda and scratch setup are emitted
only when configured. `environment.conda_env` must be absolute.

Each job accepts `name`, `branch`, `workdir`, `command`, `prepare`,
`prepare_once`, `configs`, and `matrix`. The complete
[`examples/slurm.toml`](../examples/slurm.toml) shows every property plus
Python, C++, and shell commands. Rendered files are private to the current user
(`0700`). Repeating an identical render safely reuses its files; changed files
are never overwritten.

## Direct CLI

```sh
slurm plan examples/slurm.toml
slurm plan --config slurm.toml --json
slurm plan examples/slurm.toml > slurm-plan.txt
slurm plan examples/slurm.toml --json > slurm-plan.json
slurm render slurm.toml
slurm render slurm.toml --output slurm-build --apply
slurm submit slurm.toml --apply
```

`CONFIG` is optional and defaults to `slurm.toml`; `--config` remains available
for scripts. `plan` prints every expanded command. Redirect it to export a
compact text summary; `--json` exports stable `name`, `workdir`, `branch`,
`command`, and `values` fields for each expanded job. `render` and `submit` print
the same plan and exact output paths before doing anything. Direct applies
require typing `APPLY`; the shared TUI confirms with `CLEAN` and then supplies
`--apply --yes`. Submission reports progress and stops on the first failed
`sbatch`.

## Exit status

`0` means planning, rendering, or submission completed. `1` means an apply was
cancelled. `2` means configuration, expansion, output, dependency, or
submission failed.
